00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:46.199
No, because I modulate my, uh, my vocabulary
so that it is easier for them to understand.

00:00:46.199 --> 00:00:52.390
So instead of saying Vos I say do and I conjugate
the way that they would be used to. So I mean,

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:58.760
I'm modulating my Spanish when I know that
I'm speaking to a non Argentinian

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:04.070
and when you like, so I think the, the double
LA like, uh, it has a Y noise.

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:08.680
Oh yeah. I don't do that. I don't do that.
I don't, I don't do that. That's too ingrained.

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:10.880
I can't do that.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:16.720
See, for me, like I like, I have a hard time
understanding the Argentinian accent just

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:22.020
because of that, because it's such a different
noise than the, the wind noise that I'm used

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:23.020
to.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:26.110
That's interesting. Yeah. I'm watching a show
on Netflix right now called in English. They've

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:31.330
translated it as money heist. It's a Spanish
show like from Spain and there's a lot of

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:36.070
Spanish slang and fast talking and I have
trouble understanding that like I put the

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:39.670
Spanish subtitles on to be able to see what
they're saying cause that's very unclear for

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:43.560
me. But like Argentinian Spanish, just because
it's what I grew up around is super easy to

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:44.560
understand.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.460
Yeah, that's really interesting to hear. Okay.
Thank you for answering my question, David.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:49.770
